    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras face a high-upside slate today, anchored by **Junior Caminero** (81.92) and **Yordan Alvarez** (78.39). **Junior Caminero** presents the premier play, benefiting from a massive +19% SP skill boost against Sugano, further amplified by a +14% park factor that pushes his xwOBA profile into the elite tier. Similarly, **Yordan Alvarez** remains a locked-in start against Taillon; despite a slight park penalty, his elite 0.476 xwOBA and favorable platoon split make him the engine of the offense. We are also tracking **Trea Turner** (71.83) closely; he is an essential play due to the stolen base environment created by Jake Irvin\u2019s slow delivery, which maximizes **Trea Turner**'s elite sprint speed efficiency.\n\nIn terms of high-leverage decisions, the case for **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (70.00) stands out. Although he faces a significant platoon disadvantage against Lauer (-15%), his massive +19% SP skill bump and +10% order incentive as the presumed leadoff hitter make him an efficient play. Conversely, **Nelson Velazquez** (49.55) was the strongest candidate to crack the lineup from the bench. He was ultimately squeezed out by the depth of our outfield and the fact that his matchup against Will Warren offered less categorical scarcity than the high-SB potential provided by **Trea Turner** and our primary speed options.\n\nRisk management remains critical, particularly with **Christian Yelich** (51.36) and **Lars Nootbaar** (51.08), both of whom are facing difficult SP matchups. **Christian Yelich** specifically encounters Paul Skenes, an elite arm that induces a -19% SP skill penalty, though we are rostering him for his dynamic platoon edge. We are also closely monitoring the status of **Chase Meidroth**, **Kyle Schwarber**, **Carson Benge**, and **Lars Nootbaar**; their \"Lineup Pending\" status introduces volatility. Should these players shift out of their projected order spots, their daily efficiency scores will require immediate 